Usage

Supported architectures: amd64, arm64, armv7, hppa, i386, macppc, octeon,
powerpc64, riscv64, and sparc64.

Run a VM:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-d directory] [-f image] [-p port] <arch> \
        [qemu args ...]

Boot installation media with the target VM disk attached:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-k] [-d directory] [-f image] \
        -m (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]

Autoinstall or autoupgrade over TFTP:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-k] [-d directory] [-f image] \
        -i (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]
    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-k] [-d directory] [-f image] \
        -u (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]

Use -m for hppa, macppc, powerpc64, and sparc64; -i and -u are available
for the remaining architectures.

Use -s for a snapshot or -R version for a release. The optional
installurl selects a distribution mirror or local directory.

Use -n to print commands instead of running them.

Use -k to bypass signature verification on the host.

Use -p port in normal run mode to forward a host port to the guest's SSH
port; no host SSH port forwarding is bound by default.

Use -f image before <arch> to select the qcow2 disk in any mode;
the default is <arch>.qcow2 in working directory.

Use -d directory before <arch> to change the working directory.
Relative paths, including -f, are resolved there; configuration and
bundled firmware are loaded from beside obsd.sh.

DISK_SIZE env sets the size of new VM disks; the default is 128G.


Installation configuration

All installation modes serve install.conf and disklabel from beside
obsd.sh through the bundled HTTP server; upgrade.conf uses the same
answers. Temporary installation files are removed when QEMU exits.

When site/ exists beside obsd.sh, bsdtar builds site.tgz using
site.perms, which specifies archive paths, ownership, modes, and source
files relative to site/ in mtree syntax. For example:

    #mtree
    /set type=file uid=0 gid=0 mode=0644
    ./etc type=dir mode=0755
    ./etc/example.conf mode=0600 contents=etc/example.conf
    ./install.site mode=0755 contents=install.site


Architecture limits

On armv7, the MSDOS boot partition appears as i: in the disklabel;
autopartitioning has room for at most 14 OpenBSD partitions.

On macppc, -m boots cd*.iso; use one CPU and at most 2G of RAM.

On octeon, use four CPUs.


Image maintenance

Shrink a qcow2 image:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-d directory] [-f image] -z <arch> \
        [qemu-img convert args ...]

Add -c after <arch> to create a compressed qcow2 image. To reclaim
unused space, first boot the guest with /bsd -s and zero free space:

    fsck -p && mount -uw / && mount -a -t ffs &&
        mount -t ffs | while read dev on mp rest; do
            dd if=/dev/zero of=$mp/.zero bs=1m
            rm -f $mp/.zero
        done
    sync
    halt
